{
  // B-009: `latestFeature` is deliberate, and it is not what makes two machines disagree about the
  // build. It accepts any 10.0.x SDK (the floor is 10.0.100, feature band 1xx or newer), which is what
  // lets the guide's one prerequisite - ".NET 10 SDK, 10.0.100 or newer" - be true on a machine that
  // has only ever installed the current installer build. Narrowing it to `latestPatch` would require a
  // 10.0.1xx SDK and refuse a fresh Windows box that ships with 10.0.4xx, which is the defect B-009 is
  // about, inverted.
  //
  // What it does not do is decide the NativeAOT compiler, and that is the field's whole story. The
  // compiler used to come from this SDK's own `KnownILCompilerPack` (10.0.11 on one machine, 10.0.14
  // on another), so two machines compiled the shipped worker with two compilers - the 10.0.11 one
  // carrying GHSA-p3jp-9v83-76w9. The repository now derives the compiler from the SDK's runtime band
  // (`ZenkinsILCompilerVersion` in Directory.Build.targets) instead of hard-coding it, because the
  // compiler and `Microsoft.NETCore.App.Runtime.*` must be the same patch or ILC refuses to publish; a
  // constant pin is wrong on any machine whose SDK is on a different 10.0.x patch, which is exactly how
  // pinning 10.0.14 turned three CI runners red in the PublishAot segment. ZENKINS0002/ZENKINS0003
  // fail the build when the pair disagrees or falls below the patched floor. Revisit this field only
  // if the SDK itself (not a package it injects) turns out to change build output.
  "sdk": {
    "version": "10.0.100",
    "rollForward": "latestFeature"
  },
  "test": {
    "runner": "Microsoft.Testing.Platform"
  }
}
